		heading: 'What “reproof” means',
		bodyHtml: `<p>Reproof is <strong>correction that is meant to bring someone back to the right way</strong>. In the Bible it is not scorn or a put-down; it is a pointing-out of what is wrong so that it can be put right. The old word is the King James Version&rsquo;s. Father&rsquo;s Heart Bible, translating the same Hebrew and Greek into plain English, usually says &ldquo;correction,&rdquo; &ldquo;rebuke&rdquo; or &ldquo;discipline,&rdquo; so when you look for &ldquo;reproof&rdquo; in this Bible you are looking for those words.</p><p>The Bible&rsquo;s own reason for it is love: &ldquo;for our Father disciplines the one his heart loves, just as a father disciplines the son he delights in.&rdquo; (Proverbs 3:12).</p>`,
	},
	{
		id: 'hebrew-and-greek',
		heading: 'The Hebrew and Greek behind it',
		bodyHtml: `<p>The Hebrew is <em>t&ocirc;wkechah</em> (also spelled <em>t&ocirc;wkachath</em>, Strong&rsquo;s H8433). Blue Letter Bible defines it as chastisement or, figuratively and by words, correction and proof, and notes that the King James Version translates it &ldquo;reproof&rdquo; 14 times and &ldquo;rebuke&rdquo; 7 times. The Brown-Driver-Briggs lexicon lists &ldquo;rebuke, correction&rdquo; and &ldquo;reproof, chiding,&rdquo; and its Proverbs entries pair it with <em>musar</em>, discipline, so the two words travel together.</p><p>In the New Testament the Greek noun is <em>elenchos</em> (Strong&rsquo;s G1650), which Blue Letter Bible glosses as proof or conviction; the King James Version uses &ldquo;reproof&rdquo; for it in 2 Timothy 3:16. Its verb, <em>elench&#333;</em> (G1651), means to expose a fault, to rebuke or to convict, and the King James Version renders it &ldquo;reprove&rdquo; 6 times, &ldquo;rebuke&rdquo; 5 times and &ldquo;convince&rdquo; 4 times. Both halves of the Bible mean the same thing: showing someone the truth about themselves.</p>`,
	},
	{
		id: 'scripture-does-it',
		heading: 'Scripture itself is how He reproves',
		bodyHtml: `<p>Paul says where reproof comes from: &ldquo;All Scripture is breathed out by our Father&mdash;useful for teaching, exposing wrong, correcting, and training in right living,&rdquo; (2 Timothy 3:16). In the King James Version, &ldquo;exposing wrong&rdquo; is the word &ldquo;reproof.&rdquo; Notice the order: teaching first, then exposing wrong, correcting and training. Reproof is one step in a path whose end is &ldquo;so that the person of our Father may be complete, equipped for every good work&rdquo; (2 Timothy 3:17).</p><p>The Bible even calls a good reproof part of how we are led: &ldquo;For the commandment is a lamp and the teaching a light, and the reproofs of discipline are the way of life,&rdquo; (Proverbs 6:23). It is one of the two places where this Bible prints the word itself.</p>`,
	},
	{
		id: 'a-fathers-correction',
		heading: 'The Father’s loving correction',
		bodyHtml: `<p>The deepest answer in the Bible is that reproof is how a good father treats a child he loves. &ldquo;My son, do not despise the discipline of our Father or be weary of his correction, for our Father disciplines the one his heart loves&rdquo; (Proverbs 3:11-12). The writer of Hebrews builds a whole argument on it: &ldquo;Our Father is treating you as sons; for what son is there whom his father does not discipline?&rdquo; (Hebrews 12:7). A father who never corrected would be one who did not care.</p><p>It is honest about the cost, too: &ldquo;For the moment all discipline seems painful rather than pleasant, but later it yields the peaceful fruit of righteousness to those who have been trained by it.&rdquo; (Hebrews 12:11). And the reason is given a verse earlier: &ldquo;he disciplines us for our good, so we may share his holiness.&rdquo; (Hebrews 12:10). Correction from our Father is never a verdict that He is done with us; it is the opposite.</p>`,
	},
	{
		id: 'receiving-reproof',
		heading: 'Receiving reproof: the wise and the foolish',
		bodyHtml: `<p>Proverbs treats how we take reproof as a dividing line. &ldquo;Whoever loves discipline loves knowledge, but whoever hates correction is senseless.&rdquo; (Proverbs 12:1). &ldquo;The ear that listens to life-giving correction will dwell among the wise.&rdquo; (Proverbs 15:31), and &ldquo;whoever listens to correction gains understanding.&rdquo; (Proverbs 15:32). The refusal of it is the mark of the fool, as our page on <a href="/meaning/folly/" style="color: var(--color-brand);" class="hover:underline">what folly means</a> shows, while the willingness to hear it is a large part of <a href="/meaning/wisdom/" style="color: var(--color-brand);" class="hover:underline">wisdom</a>.</p><p>The warning side is plain as well: &ldquo;Whoever is often corrected yet refuses to listen will suddenly be broken beyond healing.&rdquo; (Proverbs 29:1). Reproof is a gift, and a gift can be refused.</p>`,
	},
	{
		id: 'exhortation',
		heading: 'Reproof and exhortation',
		bodyHtml: `<p>The two words are often paired. Paul tells Timothy: &ldquo;Preach the word; stand ready in season and out; correct, rebuke, and encourage, with complete patience and teaching.&rdquo; (2 Timothy 4:2). To <strong>exhort</strong> is to urge and encourage someone toward the right way, and in this Bible the word reads &ldquo;encourage.&rdquo; Reproof says what is wrong; exhortation says &ldquo;come this way.&rdquo; Both are done &ldquo;with complete patience,&rdquo; and both are meant to build up.</p>`,
	},
];

